entish
======

WARNING: AI vibe-coded software.

Scripting language and interactive shell for my next 10 years.
Ideas stolen from many different languages.

LEARN.md is a short tour, STREAMS.md the two-stream model, DESIGN.md the
whole design (TODO marks what is not built yet).

Lang
====

Byte pipelines
--------------

    # standard unix byte pipelines
    $ ls *.mp4 | lines |% { |f|             # | lines: stdout -> one value per line
        # extract audio
        ffmpeg -i $f -vn -c:a copy $f.m4a
    }


The item: $_ (from Hack and Perl)
---------------------------------

    # the stage after |% runs once per value; the value goes where $_ is,
    # or in as the last arg. An external gets it only through $_
    $ ...['a' 'bb'] |% len           # len $_
    ▶ 1
    ▶ 2
    $ ls *.mp4 | lines |% ffmpeg -i $_ -vn -c:a copy $_.m4a
    $ ls | lines |% mv $_ $_.bak
    $ ...[1 2 3] |% $_ * 2 |> list
    ▶ [2 4 6]


Two streams (from elvish / rash)
--------------------------------

    # every command emits output (bytes) and values (data)
    # | passes output, |> and |% pass values; nothing converts between them
    $ ls | lines            # lines turns output into values
    ▶ main.go
    ▶ go.mod
    $ ls | lines |> count   # |> gives the whole stream to one stage
    ▶ 2

    # per-item logic on real values: files over 1000 bytes
    # (bash: while read f; do [ "$(stat -c %s "$f")" -gt 1000 ] && ...)
    $ ls | lines |% { |f| if (num $(stat -c %s $f)) > 1000 { $f } }
    ▶ big.bin

    # a list stays a list: no word splitting, names with spaces are safe
    # (bash: mapfile -t, or find -print0 | xargs -0)
    $ let mp4 = (ls *.mp4 | lines |> list)
    $ ls -1 ...$mp4
    a b.mp4
    c.mp4

    # values stream through fns too: each in a fn reads the stream from |>
    $ fn tag { each { "x$it" } }
    $ tail -f log | lines |> tag |> print   # each line as it arrives


Functional, no loops
--------------------

    # a fn writes output and emits values; each operator takes one
    $ fn build { go build -o app .; 'app' }   # output to stdout, value 'app'
    $ let bin = (build)                      # ( ) takes the values, $(build) the output
    $ let bin = build                        # compile error: write (build) or $(build)

    # |% { } maps and filters; |> list collects, ...$xs spreads
    $ ...[1 2 3 4 5] |% { if $it % 2 == 1 { $it * $it } } |> list
    ▶ [1 9 25]

    # recursion instead of loops; a generator is a fn that emits
    $ fn fact n { if $n <= 1 { 1 } else { $n * (fact ($n - 1)) } }
    $ fact 5
    ▶ 120
    $ fn evens n { if $n > 0 { evens ($n - 2); $n } }
    $ evens 6 |> list
    ▶ [2 4 6]

    # fns are values: named fns and builtins too
    $ fn double x { $x * 2 }
    $ ...[1 2 3] |% $double |> list
    ▶ [2 4 6]

    # stdlib builtins are namespaced and take their data last, for |%
    $ string:uppercase héllo
    ▶ HÉLLO
    $ 'Fun to learn, delightful to ship' |%
    ::: string:lowercase |%
    ::: string:replace ',' '' |%
    ::: string:split |> list
    ▶ ['fun' 'to' 'learn' 'delightful' 'to' 'ship']


Conditions (from ysh)
---------------------

    # a command is tested by its exit status, ( ) by its value
    $ if grep -q TODO main.go { print todo }
    $ fn big n { $n > 9 }
    $ if (big 10) { print big }
    big
    $ if big 10 { print big }       # compile error: big ends in a value, so as a
                                    # command it always succeeds; to test it: (big ...)


Rational by default (from raku)
-------------------------------

    $ 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3
    ▶ $true

    $ 1 / 3 * 4
    ▶ 4/3

    # no silent rounding; round explicitly
    $ round 4 (1 / 3)
    ▶ 0.3333


Pattern matching
----------------

    # check the shape of a value and take it apart; no arm matching raises
    $ fn g ...args {
        match $args {
          []             => git status -sb
          ['co' branch]  => git checkout $branch
          ['l' ...rest]  => git log --oneline ...$rest
          _              => git ...$args
        }
      }
    $ match [status: 'ok' n: 3] { [status: 'ok' n: n] if $n > 0 => $n; _ => 0 }
    ▶ 3


Code as text: source, src
-------------------------

    # source: a literal path, read and checked with the program
    $ source ~/.config/entish/git.ent
    # at the REPL, run it again after editing the file: fns are redefined

    # src: the source of a fn or lambda, as written
    $ fn check body { if !(call $body) { let s = (src $body); fail "check failed: $s" } }
    $ let n = 0
    $ check { $n > 0 }
    error: check failed: { $n > 0 }


Strings
-------

    # ask on the terminal, even when stdin or stdout is redirected
    $ let name = (prompt 'Name? ')
    Name? Dave
    $ print "Hello, $name!"
    Hello, Dave!

    # "..." interpolates variables only; code in a string is a compile error
    $ let today = $(date +%F)
    $ print "today $today"
    $ print "today $(date +%F)"     # error: a string interpolates variables, never code

Multiline strings (from zig)
----------------------------

    $ let name = "entish"
    $ echo (
        $\\name = "$name"
        \\print(f"hello {name}")
      ) | python3 -
    hello entish


Editor
======

- The prompt numbers each input: `[42]`. `$42` replays that input's displayed
  values as a stream: `$42 |> count`, `$42 |% { ... }`. Collect with
  `let saved = ($42 |> list)`; index with `($42 |> list)[0]`, not `$42[0]`.
  Single-value positions require exactly one saved value. Only the latest 100
  inputs are retained in this session; a named value or closure can keep results
  longer. Stdout and intermediate pipeline values are not saved.
  Failed or expired results give an error, never a rerun. Numbered references
  work only in direct REPL input, not scripts, sourced files or eval source.
- Vim by default
- Key bindings: `bind ctrl-x { print $it | wl-copy -n }` (a fn with a param
  gets the input); a str the fn returns is inserted at the cursor:
  `bind ctrl-v { $(wl-paste -n) }`; a map `[input: str]` replaces the input:
  `bind alt-q { [input: "shq add $it"] }`. Keys are `ctrl-a`..`ctrl-z` and `alt-c`.
- the bracket at the cursor and its pair are underlined; brackets in strings
  and comments don't count
- an open completion menu narrows as you type (fuzzy); descriptions from fish
  completions show in their own column
- the window title is the directory at the prompt and the command while it runs
- Ctrl+T picks files and inserts them at the cursor, each through `quote`
  (`'x y.go'`, `'it''s'`, `"a\nb"`), so any name reads back as itself.
  The picker opens below the existing input, with selections previewed in
  that input as they change. Tab marks several; Enter commits without running
  the command; Esc/Ctrl+C restores the input and cursor. File discovery uses
  `fd` asynchronously. A user `bind ctrl-t` overrides this editor mode
- `pick` is an fzf over values: `git branch | lines |> pick |% git switch $_`;
  Tab marks several, Esc picks nothing. Names with spaces or newlines are safe
- Ctrl+D picks a recent directory; Alt+Left / Alt+Right go to the previous /
  next directory of this shell, like a browser, and keep the input
- While browsing history with Ctrl+P/Up, Ctrl+P/Ctrl+N or Up/Down move between
  history entries, taking precedence over bindings. Returning to the draft
  or pressing another editing key restores ordinary bindings
- Ctrl+N opens the built-in directory navigator below the input, which stays
  as typed; the `nav` command opens it below the cursor;
  `nav ~/src` starts at a given path. Parent/current/preview columns, with
  h/j/k/l or arrow keys to move; l/Right enters a directory. Enter changes
  cwd to the displayed directory; q/Esc cancels. `/` filters (Enter finishes
  filtering), `.` toggles hidden files, Ctrl+L reloads, and Ctrl+C interrupts.
  No external file manager is needed
- with the cursor on a `$name`, its current value shows dim below the input
  (`$xs = 3 items [1 2 3]`); env vars and `$42` too, and an unset env var says
  so. A name bound in the input itself, or input that does not parse, shows
  nothing; nothing runs
- elsewhere in a call of a stdlib builtin, its signature shows there instead
  (`string:uppercase s → str: s in upper case`)
- `|` on an empty line inserts the last input before it (`echo 3 |`), to pipe
  it again

Features
========
- uncaught errors show the source line, every failed stage of the pipeline
  and the fn calls they came out of
- async direnv integration
- async completion; arguments of external commands are completed by fish (`complete -C`) when fish is on PATH; command names and file names match ignoring case when nothing matches as typed


